      "category": "cooling",
      "title": "3.0L V6 Coolant Leak and Overheating",
      "description": "The Catera 3.0L V6 develops multiple coolant leaks from the water pump, thermostat housing, and hose connections. Overheating from these leaks can warp cylinder heads.",
      "solution": "Pressure-test the cooling system and inspect the water pump, thermostat housing/coolant bridge, radiator end tanks, and all upper/lower hose connections for seepage or crusted coolant residue; on the Catera’s 3.0L V6, leaks commonly come from the water pump and plastic housing seals. Replace the failed components with updated gaskets/O-rings, install a new thermostat while the system is apart, and refill/bleed with the correct Dex-Cool coolant mixture to prevent air pockets that can trigger overheating. If the engine has overheated, check for combustion gases in the coolant and verify cylinder head flatness before returning it to service, since warped heads are a known risk. Typical repair cost ranges from about $400-$900 for hoses/thermostat housing repairs and $700-$1,300 if the water pump and related seals are replaced.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "medium",
